The '''2022 Manchester City Council election''' took place on 5 May 2022. One third of councillors on [[Manchester City Council]] were elected. This election was a part of the [[2022 United Kingdom local elections|other local elections across the United Kingdom]].

In the [[2021 Manchester City Council election|previous council election in 2021]], the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] maintained its longstanding control of the council, holding 94 of the council's 96 seats. The [[Green Party of England and Wales|Green Party]] held one of the others, and the [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] held the other.

== Background ==
=== History ===
The [[Local Government Act 1972]] created a two-tier system of metropolitan counties and districts covering [[Greater Manchester]], [[Merseyside]], [[South Yorkshire]], [[Tyne and Wear]], the [[West Midlands (county)|West Midlands]], and [[West Yorkshire]] starting in 1974. Manchester was a district of the Greater Manchester metropolitan county.<ref name=guide>{{cite book |title=Local Government in England and Wales: A Guide to the New System |year=1974 |publisher=[[HMSO]] |location=London |isbn=0-11-750847-0 |page=7 }}</ref> The [[Local Government Act 1985]] abolished the metropolitan counties, with metropolitan districts taking on most of their powers as [[metropolitan borough]]s. The [[Greater Manchester Combined Authority]] was created in 2011 and began electing the [[mayor of Greater Manchester]] from 2017, which was given strategic powers covering a region coterminous with the former Greater Manchester metropolitan county.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2011/908/made |title=The Greater Manchester Combined Authority Order 2011 |publisher=Legislation.gov.uk |access-date=2015-09-07}}</ref>

Since its formation, Manchester City Council has continuously been under [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] control. In the [[2021 Manchester City Council election|most recent council election in 2021]], Labour won 31 of the 32 seats up for election with 65.4% of the vote, and the [[Green Party of England and Wales|Green Party]] won the other seat with 11.5% of the vote across the borough. The [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservatives]] received 10.8% of the vote and the [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] won 10.4% of the vote but neither party won any seats.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Griffiths |first=Niall |date=2021-05-06 |title=The Manchester council local elections 2021 results |url=https://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/news/greater-manchester-news/what-manchester-council-local-elections-20420898 |access-date=2022-03-28 |website=Manchester Evening News |language=en}}</ref>

The [[Local Government Boundary Commission for England]] produced new boundaries for Manchester ahead of the [[2018 Manchester City Council election|2018 election]], meaning that the 2018 elections were all-out, with all councillors being elected before returning to electing by thirds. Candidates up for re-election in 2022 are those who came first in each ward in 2018.

=== Council term ===
On 7 September 2021 [[Richard Leese]] announced his retirement after 38 years as a councillor and 25 years as the council leader.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/news/greater-manchester-news/breaking-sir-richard-leese-stand-21509077|title=Sir Richard Leese to stand down as Manchester council leader after 25 years|first=Jennifer|last=Williams|work=[[Manchester Evening News]]|date= 7 September 2021|access-date=16 December 2021}}</ref> He was succeeded as leader on 1 December 2021 by [[Bev Craig]] and resigned from the council on 4 January 2022.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/news/greater-manchester-news/i-dont-want-pigeonholed-manchesters-22325143|title='I don’t want to be pigeonholed': Manchester’s new town hall leader on who she is and what comes next|first=Jennifer|last=Williams|work=[[Manchester Evening News]]|date=1 December 2021|access-date=16 December 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://secure.manchester.gov.uk/info/500329/elections/4981/the_next_election/2|title=Legal Notices {{!}} The next election {{!}} Manchester City Council|date=4 January 2022|access-date=4 February 2022}}</ref> Two by-elections have been called since the 2021 election: [[2021_Manchester_City_Council_election#Chorlton_by-election|One in Chorlton]], held on 14 October 2021, following the resignation of Matt Strong, which was won by Mathew Benham for Labour, and [[2021_Manchester_City_Council_election#Ancoats_&_Beswick_by-election|one in Ancoats & Beswick]], held on 3 February 2022, following the resignation of [[Marcia Hutchinson]], which was won by Alan Good for the [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.manchester.gov.uk/downloads/download/7399/chorlton_by-election_2021_notices|title=Chorlton By-election 2021 notices|publisher=[[Manchester City Council]]|access-date=16 December 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-manchester-59479149|title=Manchester councillor quits after racist bullying claim|work=[[BBC News]]|date=1 December 2021|access-date=16 December 2021}}</ref><ref name="ancoatsandbewswick-britainelects">{{cite tweet|user=BritainElects|number=1489386766444507140|title=Ancoats & Beswick (Manchester) council by-election result}}</ref>

